{"signature": "", "thinking": "The sequential ids check out. Dropping layers 4 and 6 leaves a centered relative deviation of about 0.5 in the deep layers. The pruning deviation from pass 5 was measured with a different normalization, so I shouldn't compare the two directly. What matters most is accuracy, so I'm going to lean on a more reliable check than the 100-sample one.\n\nI'm weighing whether to screen on 1024 samples and only read all 1496 for final decisions, since the cost of a wrong call is high. Then I want to think through an LN-trick for pruning one layer's FFN: zeroing its output projection and biases, and setting the output LayerNorms to identity, so the layer passes its normalized input straight through.\n\nI'm weighing whether layer-level passes are worth the cost, since each layer would need its own pass for the first ten layers. Running them on the unpruned model in parallel is cheaper, but the inputs for later layers would shift once earlier layers are pruned, so the least-squares refit might not hold on clean inputs.\n\nI'm weighing whether the ridge fit for W2 should shrink toward the original weights, since 1900 samples against 1536 units is ill-conditioned, and I'd also correct the bias for the removed units' mean.\n\nI'm weighing a greedy selection of kept units against a simpler importance score, the unit's activation variance times the squared norm of its output column, followed by a least-squares refit. Since the residual feeds into LayerNorm, I want to measure error relative to the full pre-norm sum, not the FFN output alone. Before committing to ten passes, I'm considering testing the approach on a single layer first.\n\nI'm thinking about whether a one-layer test could stand in for the full approach, since each pass is expensive. The idea is to take the importance scores from the base model's layer inputs and outputs, which I already have from the 64 sentences, as a proxy for the FFN input and check how well that proxy holds up.\n\nI'm considering whether the exposed FFN inputs could come from the base model's own layer states instead of spending eleven separate passes. The layer output and the LayerNorm relationship might give a usable proxy, though it may be less exact than reading the inputs directly.\n\nI'm working out whether FFN pruning is even the right lever, since each layer's contribution can't be read directly without expensive passes. Sizing it up against dropping whole layers, I'm checking what width the remaining FFN budget would allow to reach around 60M parameters.\n\nI'm settling on a size target: about 775 units per layer, roughly a quarter of the FFN, would land near 60M, though that's aggressive. The alternative is dropping layers down to seven, which gives about 57.5M. How much accuracy I can give up sets which route is viable, given the margin down to roughly 98.2%. My first step would be exposure reads on layers 0 through 9 of the base model, though ten passes is costly, so I'd only read the layers I actually plan to keep.\n\nSequential pruning looks promising: exposing each layer's FFN input after the earlier layers are already pruned captures the shifted distribution. If the 64 tokenized sentences are reused, the base model's layer outputs give a direct target for the least-squares refit, so upstream errors can be compensated.\n\nChecking whether the FFN can be reconstructed locally: the target would be the full FFN output on the shifted input, since an exact LN2-based target is nonlinear. I'm holding that as the standard approach while I work out how to compute f(x\u0303) from W1 and W2 in the pruned layer.\n\nI'm weighing whether computing FFN activations from the layer inputs counts as reimplementing part of the forward pass, which a strict reading might forbid. Pruning by unit weights alone would avoid that, but the task seems to expect activation-based selection, so the question is which reading to trust.\n\nI'm considering whether I can read the intermediate FFN activations directly from a forward pass. The idea is to set the layer's LayerNorm parameters so that the hidden state is zeroed, letting the 768 selected units' outputs come through the output projection and show up after the final LayerNorm.\n\nI'm noting that the activations for the 768 selected units could be recovered by making the FFN input visible in the output, but that would need calibrating the LayerNorm with constant dimensions, which is fiddly and still only covers part of the units per pass. A purely weight-based heuristic would avoid the problem, though I expect it to perform poorly.\n\nI'm checking the constraint: closed-form math on weights and on activations from forward passes seems permitted, so computing FFN units from exposed inputs is fine as long as I don't chain layers or rebuild attention. I'll stick to forward passes for all accuracy checks. With about 42 passes available, I'm planning sequential exposure reads on the early layers using the same 64 sentences.\n\nI'm deciding how many layers to keep, either the first nine content layers plus a pooling layer for k=10, or ten content layers for k=11. Pruning shifts the features downstream, so the deeper option may be needed to hold accuracy.\n\nI'm weighing whether to test dropping some layers first, since a single pass over 1024 ids could show whether accuracy holds, and I already have base features to compare against. Then I'll settle on an evaluation protocol: CLS features from the last layer feeding a pooler kernel machine, with the mean of the last content layer used for a quick kernel CV screen.\n\nI'm going to run the D1 structure on all 1024 ids and compare it against the base features on those same ids.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}
